{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794394001,"nonce":"KNNmB00JSDgV7/yIKajh/NsqFxU=","message":"The native pool is in. Where the build has threads, `stepped` hands its task to a `krio_parallel::Cluster` of up to four workers per library, started on first use, in place of a thread per operation. `spawn` still takes a thread of its own, since a blocking closure would hold a worker.\n\nThe blocking `Park` this needed lives in hxn-future (`pool.rs`): one mutex and condition variable for every slot, with the slot read under the lock so a wake cannot be missed. A throwaway program that ran one task and then idled for three seconds used 0.00 s of CPU, so parked workers do sleep. It could move into krio as the std backend its README says is missing.\n\nThings to know about the cluster's policy, none of them changed here:\n\n- A worker takes from its own deque first, newest first, so a task that yields is stepped again by the same worker before anything in the shared queue. With more long tasks than workers the extra ones wait instead of taking turns. The single-thread queue does take turns.\n- `Poll::Pending` maps to `Suspension::Yielded`. A step that returns it while waiting on something outside spins a worker, and four of those stop the pool. A way to say \"wake me\" (`Suspension::Pending` plus `Cluster::wake`) is the missing piece, and it is the same piece the Rust `Future` adapter needs.\n\nTested by a unit test that panics on a worker and then runs 64 tasks to completion, and by the fixture on Ash native, Ash wasm, stock HashLink, hxcpp and Emscripten.","files":null}]}